From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000061 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Petr Parkan Janda <petronijus@bastla.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 22:13:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] mfd: twl6030-irq: map every PIH bit, so an unclaimed one is
 not an error

On every USB cable event the Nexus Q (steelhead) logged

  twl6030_irq: Unmapped PIH ISR 20 detected

PIH bit 20 is CHRG_CTRL. phy-twl6030-usb unmasks it in INT_MSK_LINE_C
and INT_MSK_STS_C because twl6030_irq_thread() copies it onto the VBUS
bit (19) -- "VBUS status bit is not reliable for VBUS disconnect". The
copied bit 19 reaches twl6030-usb as it should. Bit 20 itself is then
dispatched too, and since the irq domain went linear, a hwirq gets a
mapping only when a consumer translates it from DT. Nothing consumes
CHARGER_INTR_OFFSET on a board without a charger (this one has no
battery and no charger driver), so irq_find_mapping() returns 0 and the
loop prints an error for an event that was handled.

The vendor 3.0 kernel this board shipped with does the same unmask and
the same copy, and stays silent: twl6030_init_irq() there gave the whole
irq_base range a descriptor (handle_simple_irq on a dummy chip), and an
interrupt nobody requested ended on a descriptor without an action.

Do the same with the domain: create the mapping of every hwirq once the
parent interrupt is requested. An unclaimed bit then reaches
handle_nested_irq(), which drops an interrupt without an action
silently. Mapping on demand still works, since a later translation finds
the existing mapping. All PIH lines are masked at that point, so no
interrupt can arrive before the mappings exist.

Signed-off-by: Petr Parkan Janda <petronijus@bastla.com>
---
 drivers/mfd/twl6030-irq.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/mfd/twl6030-irq.c b/drivers/mfd/twl6030-irq.c
index 0ca00f6..fa77b11 100644
--- a/drivers/mfd/twl6030-irq.c
+++ b/drivers/mfd/twl6030-irq.c
@@ -292,6 +292,7 @@ int twl6030_init_irq(struct device *dev, int irq_num)
 {
 	int			nr_irqs;
 	int			status;
+	int			i;
 	u8			mask[3];
 	const struct of_device_id *of_id;
 
@@ -354,6 +355,22 @@ int twl6030_init_irq(struct device *dev, int irq_num)
 	}
 
 	twl6030_irq->twl_irq = irq_num;
+
+	/*
+	 * Map every PIH bit up front, not only the ones a consumer asks for.
+	 * The PIH raises bits nobody has to request: phy-twl6030-usb unmasks
+	 * CHRG_CTRL (bit 20) because the thread above copies it onto VBUS to
+	 * catch a disconnect, and on a board without a charger driver nothing
+	 * maps CHARGER_INTR_OFFSET, so every VBUS edge logged "Unmapped PIH ISR
+	 * 20" as an error. With a mapping, a bit nobody handles is dropped by
+	 * handle_nested_irq() like any interrupt without an action -- what the
+	 * vendor kernel did, whose irq_base range gave every bit a descriptor.
+	 * All lines are still masked here, so nothing can arrive before this.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < nr_irqs; i++)
+		if (!irq_create_mapping(twl6030_irq->irq_domain, i))
+			dev_warn(dev, "could not map PIH hwirq %d\n", i);
+
 	register_pm_notifier(&twl6030_irq->pm_nb);
 	return 0;
 
-- 
